Nandin, the leader of the gaṇa-chiefs, the valorous Mahākāya,
Ghaṇṭākarṇa, Śaṅkukarṇa, Kuṇḍodara, and Mahodara;

Nandiṣeṇa, Somanandin, the gaṇa-chief Karika,
Vighna, Vināyaka, and Sarvavināyaka;
Piṅgala, Lohita, Mayūra, and Kukkuṭa;
Pañcākṣa, Vīrabhadra, Viṭpati, and Pāvakārdana;
Adityāpyāyana, Somāpyāyana,
another, Samudrāpyāya, and Pāvakāpyāyana;
Varabarhidhvaja, Viśākha, Śākha,
and the mighty Naigameṣa, possessing the strength and valor of Rudra;
and the pramathas of terrifying forms who were born of the son of the Kṛttikās [Skanda]—
they attacked the dānavas in battle, like bulls [attacking] bulls.
A tumultuous battle took place between each of them,
dreadful, like [a fight] of rutting elephants with śarabhas in the wilderness;
like great elephants against elephants, mountains against mountains,
oceans against oceans, fires against fires;
likewise, the proud [demons], enveloped by the fire of anger,
were held in check by the valiant attendants, as obstacles [are overcome] by those devoted to dharma.

761 / Ajaka, Namuci, Vyaṃsa, Ilvaka, and Vilvaka, / Vipracitti, Tuhuṇḍa, Huṇḍa, and the dānava Vakra;
Maya, Tāra, Tārakākṣa, Vidyunmālin, and Prabhañjana; / Ariṣṭa, Riṣṭa, Añjana, Rūśabha, and Kubha;
Kāla, Kālanemi, Kālakāmukha, / Saṃpāti, Ilvala, Bala, and Naraka;
and the sons of Madhu and Kaiṭabha, Dhundhu, and the dānava Mūka.
Hastin, Vipāka, Pāka, Vikarṇa, and Śambhu, / raising many weapons blazing like falling meteors, / rushed furiously against Viṭpati, roaring like lions. / With spears, stones, clubs, and thunderous arrows, / they struck him with dreadful roars, like rainclouds against a mountain. / Just as clouds, impelled by the wind, unleash a shower of water, / so the Dānavas discharged a shower of arrows upon the god. / But he who crushes hosts of enemies,
